  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/21-seahorses-and-pipefish/microphis-brachyurus-5/",
      "html": "<p>The short-tailed freshwater pipefish (Microphis brachyurus) has been described from Indonesia. For a long time it was believed to be a species with a worldwide distribution, which differed only slightly at the subspecies level in the various distribution areas. And so M. lineatus and M. aculeatus were considered as subspecies of M. brachyurus for a long time. Today all three are considered as independent species.</p><p>Some specimens of M. brachyurus have a deep red stripe on the front third of the body. It is unknown what this stripe, which can occur in both sexes, means.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/21-seahorses-and-pipefish/microphis-brachyurus-5/",
      "html": "<p>In a recent import, males could be observed during courtship. The snout and the brood pouch turn deep black in a flash. Just as fast the male can switch off the black color again.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/21-seahorses-and-pipefish/microphis-brachyurus-5/",
      "html": "<p>The males can be easily recognized by the brood pouch on their stomach.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/21-seahorses-and-pipefish/microphis-brachyurus-5/",
      "html": "<p>As an adult animal M. brachyurus lives in freshwater, where the tiny young are born. However, these drift into the sea within 2-3 days, where they live for some time in plankton, until they migrate back into freshwater. The breeding in freshwater has not yet been successful – as far as is known.</p>"
    }
  ],
